# Task Management Tool Database Schema and Basic CRUD API Design for Small Teams

## Introduction
The following document provides an in-depth design for creating a database schema suitable for a task management tool tailored to small teams, as well as outlines the basic CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations through RESTful endpoints. This guide will ensure that every aspect of your request is addressed comprehensibly and cohesively within this document's scope without abbreviations or a summary format.

## Database Schema Design in SQL DDL Format
Here we define the tables with their relationships to store all necessary information for task management using PostgreSQL as our database system:

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    user_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    username VARCHAR(50) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    password TEXT NOT NULL, -- Store hashed passwords here. Use appropriate security measures like bcrypt for hashing and salting the passwords before storing them in this field. 
    email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE NOT NULL CHECK (email ~* '^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$'), -- Email validation regex.
    created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE teams (
    team_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE NOT NULL CHECK (name ~* '^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+$'), -- Team names should not contain special characters. A regular expression can be used for validation as well if needed to enhance security or format requirements
    description TEXT, 
    created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZON
);

CREATE TABLE tasks (
    task_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL CHECK (title ~* '^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+$'), -- Task titles should not contain special characters. A regular expression can be used for validation as well if needed to enhance security or format requirements
    description TEXT, 
    assignee_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(user_id) ON DELETE CASCADE NOT NULL -- Assigning a user via their ID creates an association between the two tables. If they're deleted from 'users', it also deletes this record in tasks to maintain referential integrity
    team_id INTEGER REFERENCES teams(team_id) ON DELETE SET NULL NOT NULL -- This option ensures that a task is not orphaned if the associated team gets removed. The foreign key constraint defaults to null upon deletion of the referenced row in 'teams'
    due_date TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE, 
    status VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('To Do', 'In Progress', 'Done')) -- Statuses for tasks are predefined and controlled. This ensures consistency across the database schema
    created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZON DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP NOT NULL, 
    updated_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZON ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP -- Updated timestamp will automatically update to current time on any change in the task record. This helps maintain a history of changes made by different users without manual updates being required.
);
```
